#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120663} :activity/id #uuid "f2dd4c26-139a-5928-934f-ad8833466f2b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049088 :topic/id #uuid "d97a4040-06bd-5a66-adf2-e1405a5fc0e6" :topic/math-academy-id 1782} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120663 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121031 :tutorial/content "There is an algorithm that uses $LU$ factorization to solve systems of equations of the form \n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nFor example, let's solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince the LU factorization of $A$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nour equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ can be written as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ni.e., \n\n$$\nLU\\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we set $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the above, we have\n\n$$\nL\\mathbf{y}= \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, we can reduce the problem to the following system of matrix equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L$ and $U$ are both triangular, each of these equations is easily solvable using back and forward substitution.\n\n- First, we solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Let's assume that $[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-1 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using *forward* substitution, we get the solution\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Finally, we solve $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ We have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using *back* substitution, we get the solution\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2a20df11-b376-5315-8a2a-b65f70b54723" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3181 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d790cb21-6bf5-548a-9699-06a28ab34cad" :step/math-academy-id 17370 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120800}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119799 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119610 :question/id #uuid "9552ad30-9d46-5884-9877-18ca8842c252" :question/math-academy-id "e-4775"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7634b3ac-f9e6-514e-87ba-e78590c9fa53" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390757}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 Linear System Using LU Factorization"} :step/id #uuid "03156e06-274b-5254-b220-6c7dbdbfbea4" :step/math-academy-id 17371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120665 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119800 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119611 :question/id #uuid "24bc4954-462b-5066-83d4-8a4a87efc7b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4777"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa9fbb8-b2bc-5c93-bb07-bc8c7ef7c115" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051083}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244039} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377100}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of an Intermediate System Using the LU Fac